---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import Ornament from "../../components/Ornament.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/site.js";
import { works, cuts, voices } from "../../lib/portal-data.js";

const gapVoices = voices().filter((v) => v.gap_note);
const gapTakes = cuts().flatMap((c) =>
  c.takes.filter((t) => t.gap_note).map((t) => ({ cut: c, take: t }))
);
---

<Layout
  title="What's not here"
  description="The honest gap list: what this portal cannot show you, and why."
  current="/method"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <span class="rubric">Gaps</span>
    <h1>What&rsquo;s not here</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A reference work that only shows you what it has is misleading about the shape of its subject.
      This page is the list of things this portal cannot show you, roughly in order of how much
      their absence matters.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>1. Chrétien&rsquo;s <em>Perceval</em> — the poem that invents the Grail</h2>
    <p>
      This is the largest hole, and it is at the exact centre of the subject. Around 1180 Chrétien
      de Troyes wrote a romance in which a boy watches a bleeding lance and a <em>graal</em> carried
      through a hall and fails to ask the question that would heal a wounded king. He never says
      what the grail is. He died before finishing. Every Grail in this portal &mdash; the
      Vulgate&rsquo;s holy vessel, the Queste&rsquo;s judge, Wolfram&rsquo;s stone,
      Perlesvaus&rsquo;s weapon, Malory&rsquo;s grief &mdash; is an answer to that unfinished poem.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no clean public-domain English translation. The good modern ones are in copyright.
      So Chrétien&rsquo;s Grail is described here from the canon and never quoted, and his cell on
      the crosswalk is marked as a version without a quotation.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Reachable in part through three texts in this collection: the Welsh <em>Peredur</em>, which
      shows the same scene with no theology attached; Wolfram&rsquo;s <em>Parzival</em>, which
      completes Chrétien directly and in the opposite direction from the French; and Perlesvaus.
    </p>

    <h2>2. The Vulgate Cycle — quotable, not publishable</h2>
    <p>
      The Lancelot-Grail cycle is Malory&rsquo;s principal source and the hinge of the French
      tradition. Its only modern English translation is in copyright, so it can be quoted here
      briefly, with the translator named, and cannot be read here. The same applies to the
      cycle&rsquo;s own <em>Queste</em> and <em>Mort Artu</em> in the Vulgate&rsquo;s voice: this
      portal carries the <em>Queste</em> in a separate public-domain translation, and reaches the
      <em>Mort Artu</em> only through Malory and the English poems that share its matter.
    </p>

    <h2>3. The Middle English Mortes — searchable, not quotable</h2>
    <p>
      The alliterative <em>Morte Arthure</em> and the stanzaic <em>Le Morte Arthur</em> are
      Malory&rsquo;s own English sources for the fall, and both are public domain. But the available
      scans of the Middle English are rough enough that the quotation gate rejects them: a
      transcription that cannot be verified is not published here. They are named throughout as
      sources and carry no validated quotations of their own, which is why the alliterative
      <em>Morte</em>&rsquo;s famous vision of Fortune&rsquo;s wheel is described rather than quoted.
    </p>

    <h2>4. The Madden Gawain romances — defeated by blackletter</h2>
    <p>
      Sir Frederic Madden&rsquo;s 1839 <em>Syr Gawayne</em> contains four poems this portal would
      like to publish: the Wedding of Dame Ragnelle, the Awntyrs off Arthure, Golagros and Gawane,
      and the Carle of Carlisle. The scan is 1839 type with long s and heavy abbreviation, and it
      scores near zero against the quotation gate. Ragnelle&rsquo;s riddle and its answer are quoted
      here from a small hand-checked transcription; the rest is searchable and not quotable.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Texts acquired but not yet set</h2>
    <p>
      Several public-domain works in this collection are validated against but have not yet been
      typeset for the reader. Quotations from them already appear throughout the site; the reading
      text is simply not built yet. They are listed on <a href={withBase("/read/")}>the texts page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Ornament />

  <section>
    <h2>Every gap this portal declares, in one list</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The rule is that a voice&rsquo;s position must carry either a validated quotation or an
      explicit statement of why it doesn&rsquo;t. Silence is not permitted. These are all the places
      the second case applies.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">Voice</th><th scope="col">Why there is no quotation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {gapTakes.map(({ cut, take }) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={withBase(`/throughlines/${cut.id}/`)}>{cut.short ?? cut.title}</a></td>
              <td><a href={withBase(`/library/${take.voice}/`)}>{take.voice}</a></td>
              <td class="small">{take.gap_note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          {gapVoices.map((v) => (
            <tr>
              <td>the dossier</td>
              <td><a href={withBase(`/library/${v.id}/`)}>{v.name}</a></td>
              <td class="small">{v.gap_note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
